-- title:
--   The coupling at a line position is the window sum (round six, `couple_window`)
-- statement:
--   **The coupling at a line position is the window sum.**
--
--   The following holds (Lean statement, in the scope of module `Solutions.J6SegLn6`):
--
--   ```lean
--   theorem J6Seg.couple_window (hsort : L.Pairwise (· < ·)) (hL : ∀ x ∈ L, x < B.s) (hwK : B.w ≤ K)
--       (hKm : 2 * K + 1 ≤ L.length) (hws : 2 * B.w < B.s) (zf : ℕ → ℤ)
--       (hz : ∀ k, k ∉ L → zf k = 0) {r : ℕ} (hr : r < L.length) :
--       coupleI P T B zf (L.getD r 0) = ∑ i ∈ range (2 * K + 1),
--         fmul P (ccOf T B L K r i) ((zext K (zlOf L zf)).getD (r + i) 0)
--   ```
--
--   **Context.** Segmented inverse (S2), machine side 6: the coupling as a cyclic window
--
--   On a sorted line `L = [j₀ < … < j_{m-1}]` of positions `< s` (the near-boundary positions), with
--   `z` vanishing off `L`, the coupling `W = U z` at `j_r` only reads `z` at the line indices
--   `q` within cyclic index distance `K ≥ w` of `r` (positions within cyclic distance `w`, and the
--   line is strictly increasing). With the cyclically extended list
--   `zext = (last K of zl) ++ zl ++ (first K of zl)`, `zl = [z_{j_0}, …, z_{j_{m-1}}]`:
--
--   * **`couple_window`**: `W_{j_r} = ∑_{i ≤ 2K} fmul (cc_r)_i zext_{r+i}` (`2K+1 ≤ m`, `2w < s`);
--   * **`couple_off`**: `W_i = 0` at positions that are not near a block boundary.
